class Solution {
    public double myPow(double x, int n) {
        double result=1;
        long num=n;
        if(num< 0){  //when negative number, we can change it to positive and calculate the result,
                         //then take the reciprocal of the result
          x = 1 / x;
          num = -num;
}
        while(num>0){

            if(num%2==1){
                result*=x;
            }
            x=x*x; //when num is even, we can square x and half num, which can reduce the number of multiplications
            num=num/2;//when num is odd, we can multiply x to the result and then make num even, which can also reduce the number of multiplications

        }
        return result;

    }
}gu ides us to calculate the power of a number efficiently by using the properties of exponents. When n is even, we can express x^n as (x^(n/2))^2, which allows us to compute the result with fewer multiplications. When n is odd, we can express x^n as x * (x^(n-1)), which also reduces the number of multiplications needed. This approach significantly improves the performance of the algorithm, especially for large values of n.
// The time complexity of this algorithm is O(log n) because we are halving the exponent n at each step, which leads to a logarithmic number of multiplications. The space complexity is O(1) since we are using only a constant amount of extra space to store the result and intermediate variables.